--               Deterministic: same source in, same file out, byte for byte.
--
-- ** No row in this file was authored by hand or from memory. ** Every row
-- is a transform of the file above. A fabricated ZIP->county mapping fails
-- SILENTLY -- it resolves, returns the wrong county's rates, and puts a
-- wrong premium in front of a client with nothing downstream to catch it.
-- A missing ZIP fails loudly and harmlessly. If this file is ever extended,
-- extend it the same way: fetch, transform, verify counts, commit.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- COUNTS -- verified against the source before commit
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--   Crosswalk rows (TX)      : 2,894   <- matches independent count of
--                                         source rows with GEOID_COUNTY_20
--                                         starting '48'
--   Distinct ZCTAs           : 1,992
--   Distinct counties        : 254     <- all 254 TX counties; exact
--                                         set-match with county_reference
--                                         (V076), 0 either way
--   ZCTAs spanning >1 county : 686     (34.4% of TX ZCTAs)
--   land_area_ratio NULL     : 0
--   Max land_area_ratio      : 1.000000 (none above; clamped in generator)
--
-- ** 34% is the number to remember. ** A third of Texas ZIPs resolve to
-- more than one county, so the disambiguation path is the common path, not
-- the edge case. Anything that auto-selects is wrong one time in three.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- LIMITATION: ZCTA IS NOT ZIP -- and this affects real users
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ZCTAs are Census tabulation areas approximating USPS ZIP codes. They are
-- NOT the same set: ZCTAs omit ZIPs with no residential delivery area,
-- notably PO-box-only and single-building ZIPs. A valid USPS ZIP an agent
-- types may simply not be here.
--
-- That is a MISS, and the UI must say so honestly -- "we don't have that
-- ZIP, choose a county" -- and must never say "invalid ZIP". The user is
-- not wrong; this table is incomplete by construction.
--
-- HUD's USPS ZIP-county crosswalk is the better source (built from USPS
-- delivery data, carries a residential ADDRESS ratio). Attempted first on
-- 2026-08-01 and unobtainable: huduser.gov file paths return HTTP 202 with
-- a zero-byte body, and its API returns 401 without a registered token.
-- Swapping to HUD later replaces this file's rows; V084's table is
-- unchanged by that swap.
--
-- Note also that Texas counties legitimately carry ZIPs with non-Texas
-- prefixes -- e.g. 73949/73960 (Oklahoma prefix) in Sherman County and
-- 88430 in Dallam County. Rows are selected by COUNTY FIPS, never by ZIP
-- prefix; a prefix filter would have dropped these.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SCOPE: TEXAS ONLY
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deliberate, per ichra_flow_and_handoffs.md S5 ("Texas first, same as
-- V076"), and matching SWBD's book. This is a KNOWN gap, not a surprise:
-- a ZIP in any other state finds nothing and falls to the county dropdown.
-- Adding a state is this same script with -State XX plus that state's FIPS
-- prefix, emitted as a new V0NN data migration. No schema change, no code
-- change.
--
-- Feature : T74 ZIP intake, part 1 (data layer).
-- Prereqs : V084 (zip_county table).
-- Scope   : All instances -- reference data, inert where unused.
-- Rollback: DELETE FROM zip_county WHERE county_fips LIKE '48%';
-- =====================================================================



CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW schema_info AS
SELECT 'V085' AS version, '2026-08-01' AS updated;

-- Self-register
VALUES ('V085', 'Texas ZIP to county crosswalk data (2894 rows, Census 2020 ZCTA relationship file)', 'V085__zip_county_crosswalk_tx.sql', NOW());
